  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/c/ce/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_5.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "JJJ has sponsored his own TV program to discredit Spider-Man, which Peter Parker and various classmates watch from a bowling alley.&nbsp; But \"in another part of town,\" we see that Doctor Doom is also watching.&nbsp; As the Fantastic Four have always managed to frustrate his plans, perhaps if he allied himself with one such as Spider-Man...\n\nNow headquartered in a new lab, he uses a captured spider to relay a signal to Spider-Man, who he believes will pick up the message with his spider senses.&nbsp; It directs Spider-Man to him straight away, and Doom attempts to recruit him...unsuccessfully of course.&nbsp; After a very brief scuffle, Spider-Man escapes, and Doom prepares for Plan B.&nbsp; He blows up the \"deserted building\" he was hiding out in, making it look like Spider-Man is fleeing the scene.&nbsp; Of <i>course</i> he'll be blamed for the fire!&nbsp; But Doom is planning to find out who Spider-Man really is by devising a device that will react only to a spider's impulses and lead him directly to his prey.\n\nMeanwhile, the kids at Peter's school are laughing at Flash Thompson dressed in a Spider-Man costume to surprise Peter with.&nbsp; He doesn't know that at the same time, Dr. Doom has created a device to track Spider-Man's impulses, which he believe will lead him directly to the hero's true identity.\n\nAfter school, Flash (still in his homemade costume) prepares to jump out and scare \"puny Parker,\" but because Flash and Peter are so near each other, when Doom's device draws him to the area, Doom mistakes Flash for the real Spider-Man and captures him.\n\n\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n\n\nSoon afterward, Doom takes over the TV waves and broadcasts a message aimed at the Fantastic Four.&nbsp; He'll kill Spider-Man unless the FF disband and surrender to him!&nbsp; Peter can't believe what he's seeing on the TV -- who <i>is</i> this fake Spider-Man?&nbsp; But for some reason, Liz actually calls Peter to tell him about Flash dressed as Spider-Man (<i>why on Earth would she call Peter though?&nbsp; Go figure!</i>).\n\nPeter has no choice but to go out and hunt for Doom, figuring that to take over the TV waves required a lot of power, and sure enough finally feels \"the hostile emanations\" coming from an abandoned factory below.&nbsp; (<i>Who abandons a factory in NYC</i>?)&nbsp; And here we go!&nbsp; The narrative promises the \"gol-dangest, ding-bustedest, rip-snortin'est\" fight we've ever seen!\n\nHe attacks Doom, who realizes his error of capturing an imposter.&nbsp; The two battle for several pages, with Spider-Man constantly on the defense as Doom and his various devices gradually gain ground.&nbsp; Even Spider-Man can sense that he's barely hanging on and can't take much more, but the sudden arrival of the Fantastic Four causes Doom to realize he can't take on both Spider-Man and the FF at the same time.&nbsp; He flees the scene.&nbsp; Spider-Man rushes off because he realizes he left Aunt Man alone, leaving the arriving FF to rescue Flash Thompson.\n\nThe last panels show Peter with Aunt May, the next day at the office of the Bugle, where JJJ berates him for not having any photos to sell, while Betty Brant tells him not to worry about it - <i>she</i> thinks he's wonderful!&nbsp; And then finally at school where Flash Thompson is talking big about his \"escape\" from Doctor Doom.&nbsp; Peter's luck at school is true to form!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "A solid offering, pitting Spider-Man against Doctor. Doom, which of course seemed like a terrible mismatch (and indeed <i>was</i> in fact).&nbsp; It flowed well, had most of the elements that make this magazine good, with a few weak spots thrown in as well.\n\nQuestions are raised right from the first page, such as:\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Why was Peter at a bowling alley with his classmates?] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Since when has he ever gotten along with them?)]\n\nFlash's portrayal seemed true to form from start to finish -- and is revealed here to be a honest-to-goodness Spider-Man fan.&nbsp; And you can't help but admire J. Jonah Jameson for his attempts to make Spider-Man look bad every chance he gets -- even to the point of buying television time to air a special (and we learn on page 8 that JJJ's <i>real</i> motivation is money -- \"Spider-Man <i>sells papers</i>!\").\n\nAs for Doctor Doom, he appears almost immediately, and Stan takes the opportunity to show readers how he escaped his fall at the end of [LINK:1963:FF_17|FF #17].&nbsp; (Obviously this was essential since that was the last time readers saw him only two months ago.)\n\n[ASSET: right | SpiderCommunicator | width=21%] One of the things that surprised me was that Doom banked being able to send a signal out to Spider-Man via a captured spider.&nbsp; The Chameleon was also shown to send out a signal (back in [LINK:1962:ASM_1_asm1|ASM #1]) that Spider-Man somehow picked up, so this is twice in five issues (six if you include [LINK:1962:AMAZFANT_15_asm0|AMAZFANT #15]) that a villain has (somehow) decided that Spider-Man is able to pick up waves somehow.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=But when was it ever been stated that Spider-Man is able to communicate with spiders (a la the way Ant-Man communicates with ants)?]&nbsp; I don't <i>ever</i> recall reading anything of the sort.\n\nAnd of course... Spider-Man has no trouble <i>following the signal</i> back to its origin point.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=Ooooookay then!]\n\nFor my money, it only goes to show that, at this early point in the character's history, Stan and Steve probably hadn't locked things down on exactly how these spider-powers work.&nbsp; (And as other early Marvels show -- particularly with Thor and his Swiss Army Knife Uru Hammer over in <i>Journey Into Mystery</i>-- it's still a work-in-progress).\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut early characterization works for both Marvel heroes <i>and </i>villains, and Stan hilariously deals out the tried and true recruitment card that every megalomaniac has tucked up his sleeve, complete with the caption:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"Together, we can rule the <i>world</i>!\"] (all while secretly plotting to \"destroy him without a second thought\" -- <i>how typical!</i>)\n[ASSET: center | RuleTheWorld | width=67%]\n\nThis elicited some laughter on my part, particularly when Spider-Man retorts about how a team-up would certainly be a conversation starter with JJJ!&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n\nBut after thinking it over for... maybe a couple of <i>microseconds</i>... Spider-Man declines the offer.\n[BR] Now enemies, commence brief battle scene!&nbsp; Spider-Man gets away... for now.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=I think you've already figured out by my reactions that having Dr. Doom appear as a Spider-Man villain seems as unlikely a thing as you can imagine, and I'm trying to embrace the absurdity that was likely intended by the co-creators (at least in my opinion).&nbsp; It seems even <i>Spider-Man</i> views the whole thing as some sort of cosmic practical joke.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nHere's where I have to hand it to the co-creators since the notion of Doom mistaking Flash Thompson for the real Spider-Man because Peter was only feet away - [SPAN: severity=success; text=that was a clever twist.]\n[ASSET: center | PlotTwist | width=67%]\n\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n\n\nDoom seemed far more petty in this issue, and also I keep wondering why he's in NYC rather than in his own land, but I guess again that that element of Doom being a leader of a small nation simply hadn't been developed at the time.&nbsp; Twice in the story, we see the Doom robot.&nbsp; This might be the first time it appears, as I don't recall that Doom has had much time between his various dealings with the FF up to this point.\n\nPeter leaving his Aunt May alone in a dark house also seemed a bit over the top for him, especially as he was going out to fight Dr. Doom, so points off for that.&nbsp; Doom being able to \"tune in\" on the spider sense and <i>talk</i> to him?&nbsp; And Spider-Man sensing \"hostile emanations\" coming from a building?&nbsp; <i>Really</i>?\n\nFlash Thompson's portrayal seemed true to form from start to finish, but why was Peter out with his classmates at a bowling alley at the beginning,\n\nand why did Liz Allen call him out of the blue?&nbsp; Mystifying questions like this keep this offering from grading higher, but it was still a mostly entertaining read.\n\nMiss Brant is given a first name for the first time right on the splash page, which is not actually mentioned in the story until the last couple of panels and hint at more to come in this relationship.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nFIXME - write something\n\n<u>Final thought:</u>\n[BR] Does Doom decide to scrap his Spider-Man-tracking gadget because it seems to have failed him?\n[BR] Will we ever see it again?&nbsp; (Who can say?)",
  "ownership": "I bought a pretty decent copy of this book in the year 2000 (not part of the \"2000 collection\" though) for $245 and then paid nearly $85 more to have it professionally graded at CGC, coming back with a grade of 5.5.&nbsp; The book ultimately sold for $1009 on ebay in May, 2019, netting me a nice profit of over $515, which still ranks in my top-10 in terms of individual profit margin for a single book.&nbsp; While it may sell for more <i>today</i>, at the <i>time</i>, this was a very good price (and honestly more than I had bargained on).&nbsp; It remains one of only a handful that I grossed over one grand for.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}